> From another script, I'm calling "generateSurfaceNodeFiles" to generate files and "generateFromSurfaceNodeFiles" to reconstruct the result. The weird thing is that it all works fine if the line `cout << "BBB" << endl;` is there, but not if I remove it (it fails quickly, reading something into a void* when it shouldn't). What's going on?
**assistant**:
> You’ve hit a classic C++ stream‑formatting landmine: your serialization is *text-based*, but you’re using it in a way that assumes fixed record boundaries and clean tokenization. The `cout << "BBB" << endl;` line changes timing/ordering of I/O just enough to hide the underlying bug; when you remove it, you see the real problem.
> 
> Here’s what’s actually going on and how to fix it.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 1. Your “binary” files are really text streams
> 
> You open your files with:
> 
> ```c++
> std::ofstream binaryFile("outputnode-...", std::ios::binary);
> ...
> surface->streamInsert(binaryFile, tracker);
> out->streamInsert(binaryFile, tracker);
> ignore->streamInsert(binaryFile, tracker);
> binaryFile << endl;
> ```
> 
> But `streamInsert` uses normal text output:
> 
> ```c++
> virtual ostream& streamInsert(ostream& os, PointerTracker& tracker) const {
>     os << "HydroSurfaceNode" << endl;
>     HydroNode::streamInsert(os, tracker);
>     return os;
> }
> 
> ostream& HydroNode::streamInsert(ostream& os, PointerTracker& tracker) const {
>     latitude.streamInsert(os) << " ";
>     longitude.streamInsert(os) << " ";
>     os << nodeArea << " " << distAlong << " "
>        << distAcross << " " << slope << " " << minToFlow << " " << maxVelocity << endl;
>     os << edges.size() << endl;
>     for ( ... ) {
>         os << it->first << " ";
>         if (tracker.streamInsertPointer(os, it->second))
>             it->second->streamInsert(os, tracker);
>     }
>     return os;
> }
> ```
> 
> So the actual file layout is (schematically):
> 
> ```text
> HydroSurfaceNode\n
> [lat fields] [lon fields] nodeArea distAlong distAcross slope minToFlow maxVelocity\n
> edges_count\n
> w1 <pointer-encoding-1> [maybe nested node data...]
> w2 <pointer-encoding-2> ...
> HydroOutputNode\n
> ...
> HydroOutputNode\n
> ...
> \n
> ```
> 
> Then you read with:
> 
> ```c++
> std::ifstream binaryFile("outputnode-...", std::ios::binary);
> PointerReference tracker;
> 
> string subclass;
> getline(binaryFile, subclass);
> assert(subclass == "HydroSurfaceNode");
> 
> HydroNode* surface =
>     (HydroNode*) HydroSurfaceNode::streamExtractPointer(binaryFile, tracker);
> ```
> 
> and `streamExtractPointer` does:
> 
> ```c++
> static void* streamExtractPointer(istream& in, PointerReference& reference) {
>     HydroSurfaceNode* node =
>         new HydroSurfaceNode(0., 0., 0., 0., Measure(0., Inds::lat), Measure(0., Inds::lon));
>     node->streamExtract(in, reference);
>     return node;
> }
> ```
> 
> with `HydroNode::streamExtract`:
> 
> ```c++
> virtual istream& streamExtract(istream& in, PointerReference& reference) {
>     latitude = Measure::streamExtract(in);
>     longitude = Measure::streamExtract(in);
>     in >> nodeArea >> distAlong >> distAcross >> slope >> minToFlow >> maxVelocity;
> 
>     cout << "AAA" << endl;
>       
>     uint size;
>     double first;
>     in >> size;
>     edges.clear();
>     for (uint ii = 0; ii < size; ii++) {
>         in >> first;
>         HydroNode* second =
>             (HydroNode*) reference.streamExtractPointer(in, hydronodeConstructor);
>         edges.push_back(pair<double, HydroNode*>(first, second));
>     }
>     cout << "BBB" << endl;
>     return in;
> }
> ```
> 
> So far so good — if the file position is *exactly* right at every step.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Where it goes wrong: token boundaries + pointer encoding
> 
> The pointer tracking system is critical and opaque here, but you’re doing:
> 
> - On write: `tracker.streamInsertPointer(os, it->second)`  
>   This presumably writes:
>   - Something like an ID, *or*
>   - Some control token (“new pointer / existing pointer”), *and maybe* then calls `streamInsert` for that pointed‑to object.
> 
> - On read: `reference.streamExtractPointer(in, hydronodeConstructor)`  
>   This reads whatever `streamInsertPointer` wrote and either:
>   - Returns an existing object, or  
>   - Calls `hydronodeConstructor(in, reference)` to build a new one.
> 
> The failure you see:
> 
> > “it fails quickly, reading something into a void* when it shouldn’t”
> 
> is exactly what happens when:
> 
> - The input stream is *misaligned*: the next token you think is a pointer header is actually some numeric value, a class name string, or a newline leftover.
> - `streamExtractPointer` consumes the wrong bytes, interprets them as its own protocol, and then either:
>   - Returns garbage, or
>   - Calls your constructor with the stream already out of sync.
> 
> The weird dependence on the `cout << "BBB" << endl;` is a big red flag that the read/write protocol is *fragile*: that print statement forces a particular interleaving of operations and flushes, which happen to line up the stream so you don’t immediately hit the bug.
> 
> Remove it, the compiler or runtime reorders slightly, and you read one token too early or too late.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Likely concrete issues in your code
> 
> Several suspects combine here:
> 
> ### 3.1. Mixed “framing” — no explicit record delimiters
> 
> You are effectively writing a *stream of objects*:
> 
> - `"HydroSurfaceNode"` + its data + possibly nested nodes
> - `"HydroOutputNode"` + its data
> - `"HydroOutputNode"` + its data
> - etc.
> 
> But on read:
> 
> - You assume the first line is exactly `"HydroSurfaceNode"` and then that all subsequent reads line up nicely with how the pointers + edges were written.
> - You don’t read back the `"HydroOutputNode"` lines or their bodies at all in `generateFromSurfaceNodeFiles`; you only deserialize the surface node.
> 
> That’s safe only if:
> 
> - `PointerTracker` never recursively serializes those `out` and `ignore` nodes referenced from `surface`, or
> - Those pointers are written in a way that’s fully consumed inside `HydroNode::streamExtract`.
> 
> If `tracker.streamInsertPointer(os, it->second)` *does* cause `out` or `ignore` to be serialized inline (and `streamExtractPointer` knows to call `HydroOutputNode::streamExtractPointer` accordingly), but on read you treat *every* pointer as a `HydroNode` via `hydronodeConstructor`, then:
> 
> - You are feeding HydroOutputNode records into a constructor that expects a HydroSurfaceNode or HydroRiverNode, or vice versa.
> - Once any such parse goes off, every subsequent `reference.streamExtractPointer` is misaligned.
> 
> Your comment here shows you know this is a missing piece:
> 
> ```c++
> throw runtime_error("Need to have a extractor dictionary, and save the class names!");
> //XXX: HydroNode* node = HydroNode::streamExtractPointer(in, reference);
> ```
> 
> You don’t yet have a proper class-dispatching deserializer; but your pointer tracker may be serializing multiple subclasses anyway.
> 
> ### 3.2. Mixing `getline` and `operator>>` without strict framing
> 
> You’ve already noted in another context that mixing `operator>>` with `getline` is dangerous. Here you do:
> 
> - `getline(binaryFile, subclass);`
> - Then inside `streamExtract`, many `in >>` calls.
> 
> If any preceding write didn’t leave the file exactly at the beginning of the `"HydroSurfaceNode\n"` line, or left extra whitespace/newlines, `getline` will happily skip or consume them, and you’ll start reading `"HydroSurfaceNode"` or the next token from the wrong byte.
> 
> When the I/O scheduling changes (with or without `cout << "BBB"`), that fragile boundary can shift.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 4. Why `cout << "BBB"` “fixes” it
> 
> The print:
> 
> ```c++
> cout << "BBB" << endl;
> ```
> 
> - Forces a flush of `stdout`.
> - Prevents certain compiler optimizations or reordering across that call.
> - Changes timing and *sometimes* the way your test harness interleaves I/O with other operations (especially if using multiple threads, or if you re-open files rapidly, etc.).
> 
> In a strictly correct, fully deterministic single-threaded program with correct stream protocol, adding or removing a `cout` should not matter.
> 
> The fact that it does means:
> 
> - There’s an undefined behavior or a protocol violation (reading beyond the written tokens, interpreting wrong type, using uninitialized memory, etc.), and
> - The extra call happens to push that undefined behavior into a non-crashing regime.
> 
> In your case, it’s almost certainly due to reading the wrong tokens as pointer metadata, causing:
> 
> - A bogus `void*` from `streamExtractPointer`, or
> - A constructor being invoked on completely wrong stream position.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 5. Concrete fixes
> 
> To make this robust and independent of debug prints, you’ll want to tighten the serialization protocol.
> 
> ### 5.1. Add an explicit class tag for every node, and use it on read
> 
> Right now:
> 
> - You write `"HydroSurfaceNode"` / `"HydroRiverNode"` / `"HydroOutputNode"` at the top of each node’s serialization.
> - But `HydroNode::streamExtractPointer` is unimplemented, and your pointer deserialization always calls `hydronodeConstructor`.
> 
> You should implement:
> 
> ```c++
> /// Decide which subclass constructor to use
> void* hydronodeConstructor(istream& in, PointerReference& reference) {
>     std::string subclass;
>     std::getline(in, subclass);  // or better: read as a length-prefixed string
> 
>     if (subclass == "HydroSurfaceNode")
>         return HydroSurfaceNode::streamExtractPointer(in, reference);
>     else if (subclass == "HydroRiverNode")
>         return HydroRiverNode::streamExtractPointer(in, reference);
>     else if (subclass == "HydroOutputNode")
>         return HydroOutputNode::streamExtractPointer(in, reference);
>     else
>         throw std::runtime_error("Unknown HydroNode subclass: " + subclass);
> }
> ```
> 
> And then, *everywhere* you deserialize a `HydroNode*` via `PointerReference`, make sure it uses `hydronodeConstructor`, not some default that assumes a single type.
> 
> This way, when `streamExtractPointer` for a pointer sees a node that is actually an output node or a river node, it will:
> 
> - Read its tag (`HydroOutputNode` / `HydroRiverNode` / …),
> - Construct the right subclass,
> - Call the right `streamExtract`, aligning the stream correctly.
> 
> ### 5.2. Stop mixing arbitrary `getline` with unknown prior state
> 
> In `generateFromSurfaceNodeFiles`:
> 
> ```c++
> string subclass;
> getline(binaryFile, subclass);
> assert(subclass == "HydroSurfaceNode");
> 
> HydroNode* surface =
>     (HydroNode*) HydroSurfaceNode::streamExtractPointer(binaryFile, tracker);
> ```
> 
> This hard-codes class knowledge in two places:
> 
> - Once here (`assert(subclass == "HydroSurfaceNode")`)
> - Once inside `HydroSurfaceNode::streamExtractPointer`
> 
> Better pattern:
> 
> ```c++
> PointerReference tracker;
> HydroNode* node = (HydroNode*) hydronodeConstructor(binaryFile, tracker);
> HydroSurfaceNode* surface = dynamic_cast<HydroSurfaceNode*>(node);
> if (!surface) {
>     throw std::runtime_error("Expected HydroSurfaceNode in file but got something else");
> }
> ```
> 
> Now you are not pre‑consuming the line with `getline` separately — the constructor is responsible for reading its own type tag. That keeps reading and writing symmetric.
> 
> If you *do* want to keep the first `getline`, then your constructor must **not** read the subclass line again; they must match exactly.
> 
> ### 5.3. Consider a stricter framing protocol
> 
> Right now, all fields are whitespace-separated text. That makes exact recovery fragile if:
> 
> - You ever change formatting,
> - `Measure::streamInsert` / `streamExtract` include internal spaces or newlines,
> - The pointer tracker writes tokens that could be confused with your data.
> 
> More robust options you might like:
> 
> - Length‑prefixed blocks: `[type-tag-length][type-tag-bytes][payload-length][payload-bytes]`.
> - Or a pure binary format with fixed field layouts.
> 
> Given your existing code, the *minimal* change is:
> 
> - Ensure every serialized node always starts with a single line: the subclass name + `\n`.
> - Then insist that every constructor:
>   - Consumes that line once and only once, and
>   - Reads a fixed, known sequence of next tokens.
> 
> ---
> 
